    "thm-srswor-cluster-total-estimator-unbiased-and-variance": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-cluster-sampling-design-and-cluster-totals",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a uniform simple random sample without replacement (SRSWOR) of $m$ PSUs,\n$1\\le m\\le M$, the first-stage sample space is the set of all $m$-element\nsubsets of $[M]$, each with probability $\\binom{M}{m}^{-1}$. For $i,r\\in[M]$,\nwrite $I_i=1\\{i\\in S_1\\}$,\n$$\\pi_i^{(1)}=P_d(I_i=1),\\qquad\n\\pi_{ir}^{(1)}=P_d(I_i=I_r=1),$$\nwith $\\pi_{ii}^{(1)}=\\pi_i^{(1)}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "If $X$ is square-integrable, its **variance** is\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2].$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-covariance-bilinearity-and-symmetry",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ and $(Y_j)_{j\\in J}$ be finite families of square-integrable real random variables on one common probability space. Then covariance is symmetric, and for real scalars $a_i,b_j$,\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}\\!\\left(\\sum_{i\\in I}a_iX_i,\\sum_{j\\in J}b_jY_j\\right)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\sum_{j\\in J}a_ib_j\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,Y_j).$$\nThis includes empty sums and constant summands. On a finite probability space all real random variables are square-integrable, so the original finite-space case is included with the same centered-product covariance.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-variance-of-a-finite-sum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Equivalently, it is\n$\\sum_{i,j\\in I}\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j)$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Count one-PSU and two-distinct-PSU incidences to obtain π_i=m/M, π_ir=m(m−1)/(M(M−1)), Var(I_i)=m(M−m)/M², and Cov(I_i,I_r)=−m(M−m)/(M²(M−1)); for m=1 the pair probability is zero.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "choose",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Using E_d[I_i]=m/M, the expectation of (M/m)Σ_i I_iT_i is Σ_iT_i=T.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Center x_i=T_i−T̄, expand the variance of (M/m)Σ_iI_ix_i, use Σ_{i≠r}x_ix_r=−Σ_ix_i² and the covariances from step 1.1, and simplify to M²(1−m/M)S_T²/m.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The Statement fixes M>=2 and 1<=m<=M, forbidding an empty selected-PSU set. Proof1.1 explicitly treats m=1 by zero pair inclusion while3.1 still gives a defined cluster-total variance; singleton PSU SAMPLE is covered, not an empty sample.",
          "reviewed": {
            "upheld": true,
            "by": "owner-delegated-focused-boundary-review",
            "reason": "The Statement fixes M>=2 and 1<=m<=M, forbidding an empty selected-PSU set. Proof1.1 explicitly treats m=1 by zero pair inclusion while3.1 still gives a defined cluster-total variance; singleton PSU SAMPLE is covered, not an empty sample."
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 covers m=M, so all indicator variances vanish and step 3.1 gives zero estimator variance."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 treats m=1, where distinct pair inclusion is zero; step 3.1 still gives the stated variance."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "If all T_i are equal, S_T²=0 and the estimator is exact; the formula in step 2.1 is zero."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1–3.1 cover m=1 and m=M without dividing by M−m or a pair-inclusion probability."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1–3.1 count finitely many samples and use finite sums only; no choice axiom is used."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Proof1.1 derives inclusion moments under uniform PSU SRSWOR;2.1 and3.1 give unbiasedness and the exact finite-population variance. These formulas do not conversely characterize a PSU sampling design or its fixed cluster totals. The forward boundary would require a separate asserted biconditional, which this specific claim does not contain."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Proof1.1 derives inclusion moments under uniform PSU SRSWOR;2.1 and3.1 give unbiasedness and the exact finite-population variance. These formulas do not conversely characterize a PSU sampling design or its fixed cluster totals. The reverse boundary would infer these hypotheses or this construction from the conclusion, an implication this specific item does not assert."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "id": "two-psu-m1-and-census",
          "claim": "For M=2 and totals (0,2), direct enumeration at m=1 gives estimates 0 and 4 with variance 4; at m=2 the estimator is exact.",
          "result": "Here S_T^2=2, so the formula gives 4 at m=1 and 0 at m=2, matching direct enumeration."
        }
      ],
      "finite_smoke": []
    },
    "thm-equal-cluster-size-design-effect": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-cluster-sampling-design-and-cluster-totals",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let the finite element population be partitioned into\nnonempty finite sets\n$$\\mathcal U=\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{M}U_i,$$\nwhere $M\\ge1$ and $U_i$ is the set of secondary sampling units (SSUs) in the\n$i$th primary sampling unit (PSU).",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "If $X$ is square-integrable, its **variance** is\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-covariance-bilinearity-and-symmetry",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ and $(Y_j)_{j\\in J}$ be finite families of square-integrable real random variables on one common probability space. Then covariance is symmetric, and for real scalars $a_i,b_j$,\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}\\!\\left(\\sum_{i\\in I}a_iX_i,\\sum_{j\\in J}b_jY_j\\right)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\sum_{j\\in J}a_ib_j\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,Y_j).$$\nThis includes empty sums and constant summands. On a finite probability space all real random variables are square-integrable, so the original finite-space case is included with the same centered-product covariance.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-variance-of-a-finite-sum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Equivalently, it is\n$\\sum_{i,j\\in I}\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Nonnegative variances of a within-PSU difference and the within-PSU sum force ρ≤1 and ρ≥−1/(m_c−1), respectively.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "A cluster mean averages m_c SSU outcomes; covariance expansion gives variance σ²[1+(m_c−1)ρ]/m_c.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Independence across PSUs divides the single-cluster-mean variance by k; the no-FPC element reference has variance σ²/(km_c).",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "A1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Divide by the positive element-reference variance to obtain DEFF=1+(m_c−1)ρ; at ρ=0 both variances remain σ²/(km_c)>0.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The superpopulation comparison has k>=1 complete PSUs and m_c>=2 elements in each, with sigma^2>0. Proof2.1 averages k cluster means and k m_c independent reference draws; no empty averaging family or zero reference denominator is admitted.",
          "reviewed": {
            "upheld": true,
            "by": "owner-delegated-focused-boundary-review",
            "reason": "The superpopulation comparison has k>=1 complete PSUs and m_c>=2 elements in each, with sigma^2>0. Proof2.1 averages k cluster means and k m_c independent reference draws; no empty averaging family or zero reference denominator is admitted."
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 3.1 checks ρ=0: DEFF=1 while both variances equal σ²/(km_c)>0."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement assumes m_c≥2 so that a within-PSU pair and its correlation are defined."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement assumes σ²>0; if σ²=0 both compared variances vanish and their ratio is undefined."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 gives −1/(m_c−1)≤ρ≤1; substitution gives DEFF 0 and m_c at the two covariance endpoints."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1–3.1 use finite sums of square-integrable variables only; no choice axiom is used."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Independent equal-size exchangeable-covariance PSUs and the no-FPC marginal reference imply DEFF=1+(mc-1)rho in3.1. The ratio does not identify the entire within-cluster distribution or give a converse to those model assumptions. The forward boundary would require a separate asserted biconditional, which this specific claim does not contain."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Independent equal-size exchangeable-covariance PSUs and the no-FPC marginal reference imply DEFF=1+(mc-1)rho in3.1. The ratio does not identify the entire within-cluster distribution or give a converse to those model assumptions. The reverse boundary would infer these hypotheses or this construction from the conclusion, an implication this specific item does not assert."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "id": "rho-zero-boundary",
          "claim": "At ρ=0, Var(Ȳ_C)=Var(Ȳ_E)=σ²/(km_c).",
          "result": "Direct substitution gives DEFF=1 with positive variance σ²/(km_c), so zero ICC is not zero sampling error."
        }
      ],
      "finite_smoke": []
