    "thm-two-stage-expansion-total-unbiasedness-and-variance": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **sigma-algebra on $X$** is an algebra of subsets\n$\\mathcal A$ ([[def-algebra-of-subsets]]) that is closed under countable unions:\nwhenever $(A_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ is a sequence in $\\mathcal A$,\n$$\\bigcup_{n\\in\\mathbb N}A_n\\in\\mathcal A.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-conditional-expectation-given-a-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)$ be a probability space, $\\mathcal G\\subseteq\\mathcal F$ a sub-sigma-algebra, and $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ integrable. A **conditional-expectation version of $X$ given $\\mathcal G$** is a real, $\\mathcal G$-measurable, integrable function $Y$ such that $\\int_A Y\\,dP=\\int_A X\\,dP$ for every $A\\in\\mathcal G$. No completeness of $\\mathcal G$ is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-conditional-expectation-as-an-ae-class",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC for the supplied existence theorem. Write\n$E[X\\mid\\mathcal G]$ for the unique class in\n$L^1(\\Omega,\\mathcal G,P|_{\\mathcal G})$ consisting of\nconditional-expectation versions of $X$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-conditional-variance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. For real $X\\in L^2(P)$ define\n$\\operatorname{Var}(X\\mid\\mathcal G)=E[(X-E[X\\mid\\mathcal G])^2\\mid\\mathcal G]$\nas an almost-sure class.",
          "uses": [
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-conditional-variance-decomposition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. For real $X\\in L^2(P)$, $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[\\operatorname{Var}(X\\mid\\mathcal G)]+\\operatorname{Var}(E[X\\mid\\mathcal G])$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "If $X$ is square-integrable, its **variance** is\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-covariance-bilinearity-and-symmetry",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ and $(Y_j)_{j\\in J}$ be finite families of square-integrable real random variables on one common probability space. Then covariance is symmetric, and for real scalars $a_i,b_j$,\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}\\!\\left(\\sum_{i\\in I}a_iX_i,\\sum_{j\\in J}b_jY_j\\right)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\sum_{j\\in J}a_ib_j\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,Y_j).$$\nThis includes empty sums and constant summands. On a finite probability space all real random variables are square-integrable, so the original finite-space case is included with the same centered-product covariance.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-variance-of-a-finite-sum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Equivalently, it is\n$\\sum_{i,j\\in I}\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-two-stage-cluster-sampling-and-expansion-estimator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Given the selected PSU set $S_1$, the second-stage samples\n$(S_i)_{i\\in S_1}$ are independent, with each $S_i$ drawn from $p_{2i}$,\nindependently of the identities of other selected PSUs. The joint law is\n$$P_d\\bigl(S_1,(S_i)_{i\\in S_1}\\bigr)\n=p_1(S_1)\\prod_{i\\in S_1}p_{2i}(S_i).$$\nIt is a probability mass function: summing over the second-stage supports\nfirst gives $p_1(S_1)$ for each $S_1$, and summing these values gives $1$.\nFor a function $g$ of the joint outcome, write\n$$E_1[h]=\\sum_{S_1\\in\\mathcal S_1}p_1(S_1)h(S_1),\\qquad\nE_2[g\\mid S_1=s]=\\sum_{(S_i)_{i\\in s}}\n\\left(\\prod_{i\\in s}p_{2i}(S_i)\\right)g\\bigl(s,(S_i)_{i\\in s}\\bigr).$$\nFor a first-stage function $h$, define\n$\\operatorname{Var}_1(h)=E_1[(h-E_1h)^2]$. For a function $g$ of the full\nsecond-stage outcome and fixed $S_1=s$, define\n$$\\operatorname{Var}_2(g\\mid S_1=s)\n=E_2[(g-E_2[g\\mid S_1=s])^2\\mid S_1=s].$$\nThe symbol $\\operatorname{Var}_{2i}$ denotes centered-square variance under\n$p_{2i}$, and $\\operatorname{Var}_d$ denotes centered-square variance under\nthe joint law. These use the variance definition in\n[[def-moments-variance-and-covariance]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "def-two-stage-cluster-sampling-and-expansion-estimator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For the first stage, put\n$$\\pi_i^{(1)}=P_d(i\\in S_1),\\qquad\n\\pi_{ir}^{(1)}=P_d(i,r\\in S_1).$$\nFor the second-stage design in PSU $i$, put\n$$\\pi_{j\\mid i}^{(2)}=P_{2i}(j\\in S_i),\\qquad\n\\pi_{j\\ell\\mid i}^{(2)}=P_{2i}(j,\\ell\\in S_i).$$\nThe stage-specific covariance differences are\n$$\\Delta_{ir}^{(1)}=\\pi_{ir}^{(1)}-\\pi_i^{(1)}\\pi_r^{(1)},\\qquad\n\\Delta_{j\\ell\\mid i}^{(2)}=\\pi_{j\\ell\\mid i}^{(2)}-\n\\pi_{j\\mid i}^{(2)}\\pi_{\\ell\\mid i}^{(2)}.$$\n\nIf $\\pi_i^{(1)}>0$ for every PSU and $\\pi_{j\\mid i}^{(2)}>0$ for every SSU,\nthe within-PSU expansion total and the two-stage expansion total are defined by\n$$\\widehat T_i=\\sum_{j\\in S_i}\\frac{y_{ij}}{\\pi_{j\\mid i}^{(2)}},\\qquad\n\\widehat T=\\sum_{i\\in S_1}\\frac{\\widehat T_i}{\\pi_i^{(1)}}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2",
            "3.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "def-two-stage-cluster-sampling-and-expansion-estimator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a function $g$ of the joint outcome, write\n$$E_1[h]=\\sum_{S_1\\in\\mathcal S_1}p_1(S_1)h(S_1),\\qquad\nE_2[g\\mid S_1=s]=\\sum_{(S_i)_{i\\in s}}\n\\left(\\prod_{i\\in s}p_{2i}(S_i)\\right)g\\bigl(s,(S_i)_{i\\in s}\\bigr).$$\nFor a first-stage function $h$, define\n$\\operatorname{Var}_1(h)=E_1[(h-E_1h)^2]$. For a function $g$ of the full\nsecond-stage outcome and fixed $S_1=s$, define\n$$\\operatorname{Var}_2(g\\mid S_1=s)\n=E_2[(g-E_2[g\\mid S_1=s])^2\\mid S_1=s].$$\nThe symbol $\\operatorname{Var}_{2i}$ denotes centered-square variance under\n$p_{2i}$, and $\\operatorname{Var}_d$ denotes centered-square variance under\nthe joint law. These use the variance definition in\n[[def-moments-variance-and-covariance]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.2",
            "3.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "For each selected PSU, the second-stage HT total has conditional expectation Σ_j y_ij=T_i because E[J_ij|S_1]=π^(2)_j|i.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F10",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Expand each within-PSU HT variance as a finite covariance sum and substitute Cov(J_ij,J_iℓ)=Δ^(2,i)_jℓ; only positive first-order probabilities appear in denominators.",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
